msgid ""
"Users need to implement a single function to capture the CF table at startup:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:385
msgid "Tracing Stack Depth"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:387
msgid ""
"With `-fsanitize-coverage=stack-depth` the compiler will track how much "
"stack space has been used for a function call chain. Leaf functions are not "
"included in this tracing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:391
msgid ""
"The maximum depth of a function call graph is stored in the thread-local "
"`__sancov_lowest_stack` variable. Instrumentation is inserted in every non-"
"leaf function to check the frame pointer against this variable, and if it is "
"lower, store the current frame pointer. This effectively inserts the "
"following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:405
msgid ""
"If `-fsanitize-coverage-stack-depth-callback-min=N` (where `N > 0`) is also "
"used, the tracking is delegated to a callback, "
"`__sanitizer_cov_stack_depth`, instead of adding instrumentation to update "
"`__sancov_lowest_stack`. The `N` of the argument is used to determine which "
"functions to instrument. Only functions estimated to be using `N` bytes or "
"more of stack space will be instrumented to call the tracing callback. In "
"the case of a dynamically sized stack, the callback is unconditionally added."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:414
msgid ""
"The callback takes no arguments and is responsible for determining the stack "
"usage and doing any needed comparisons and storage. A roughly equivalent "
"implementation of `__sancov_lowest_stack` using the callback would look like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:428
msgid "Gated Trace Callbacks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:430
msgid ""
"Gate the invocation of the tracing callbacks with `-sanitizer-coverage-gated-"
"trace-callbacks`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:433
msgid ""
"When this option is enabled, the instrumentation will not call into the "
"runtime-provided callbacks for tracing, thus only incurring in a trivial "
"branch without going through a function call."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:437
msgid ""
"It is up to the runtime to toggle the value of the global variable in order "
"to enable tracing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:440
msgid "This option is only supported for trace-pc-guard and trace-cmp."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:442
msgid ""
"Disabling instrumentation with `__attribute__((no_sanitize(\"coverage\")))`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:444
msgid ""
"It is possible to disable coverage instrumentation for select functions via "
"the function attribute `__attribute__((no_sanitize(\"coverage\")))`. Because "
"this attribute may not be supported by other compilers, it is recommended to "
"use it together with `__has_feature(coverage_sanitizer)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:449
msgid "Disabling instrumentation without source modification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:451
msgid ""
"It is sometimes useful to tell SanitizerCoverage to instrument only a subset "
"of the functions in your target without modifying source files. With `-"
"fsanitize-coverage-allowlist=allowlist.txt` and `-fsanitize-coverage-"
"ignorelist=blocklist.txt`, you can specify such a subset through the "
"combination of an allowlist and a blocklist."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:457
msgid ""
"SanitizerCoverage will only instrument functions that satisfy two "
"conditions. First, the function should belong to a source file with a path "
"that is both allowlisted and not blocklisted. Second, the function should "
"have a mangled name that is both allowlisted and not blocklisted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:462
msgid ""
"The allowlist and blocklist format is similar to that of the sanitizer "
"blocklist format. The default allowlist will match every source file and "
"every function. The default blocklist will match no source file and no "
"function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:466
msgid ""
"A common use case is to have the allowlist list folders or source files for "
"which you want instrumentation and allow all function names, while the "
"blocklist will opt out some specific files or functions that the allowlist "
"loosely allowed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:470
msgid "Here is an example allowlist:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:481
msgid "And an example blocklist:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:490
msgid ""
"The use of `*` wildcards above is required because function names are "
"matched after mangling. Without the wildcards, one would have to write the "
"whole mangled name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:493
msgid ""
"Be careful that the paths of source files are matched exactly as they are "
"provided on the clang command line. For example, the allowlist above would "
"include file `bar/b.cpp` if the path was provided exactly like this, but "
"would it would fail to include it with other ways to refer to the same file "
"such as `./bar/b.cpp`, or `bar\\b.cpp` on Windows. So, please make sure to "
"always double check that your lists are correctly applied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:500
msgid "Default implementation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:502
msgid ""
"The sanitizer run-time (AddressSanitizer, MemorySanitizer, etc) provide a "
"default implementations of some of the coverage callbacks. You may use this "
"implementation to dump the coverage on disk at the process exit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:533
msgid ""
"Every time you run an executable instrumented with SanitizerCoverage one `*."
"sancov` file is created during the process shutdown. If the executable is "
"dynamically linked against instrumented DSOs, one `*.sancov` file will be "
"also created for every DSO."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:538
msgid "Sancov data format"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:540
msgid ""
"The format of `*.sancov` files is very simple: the first 8 bytes is the "
"magic, one of `0xC0BFFFFFFFFFFF64` and `0xC0BFFFFFFFFFFF32`. The last byte "
"of the magic defines the size of the following offsets. The rest of the data "
"is the offsets in the corresponding binary/DSO that were executed during the "
"run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:545
msgid "Sancov Tool"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:547
msgid ""
"A simple `sancov` tool is provided to process coverage files. The tool is "
"part of LLVM project and is currently supported only on Linux. It can handle "
"symbolization tasks autonomously without any extra support from the "
"environment. You need to pass .sancov files (named `<module_name>.<pid>."
"sancov` and paths to all corresponding binary elf files. Sancov matches "
"these files using module names and binaries file names."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:590
msgid "Coverage Reports"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:592
msgid "**Experimental**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:594
msgid ""
"`.sancov` files do not contain enough information to generate a source-level "
"coverage report. The missing information is contained in debug info of the "
"binary. Thus the `.sancov` has to be symbolized to produce a `.symcov` file "
"first:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:603
msgid ""
"The `.symcov` file can be browsed overlaid over the source code by running "
"`tools/sancov/coverage-report-server.py` script that will start an HTTP "
"server."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:607
msgid "Output directory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../SanitizerCoverage.md:609
msgid ""
"By default, .sancov files are created in the current working directory. This "
"can be changed with `ASAN_OPTIONS=coverage_dir=/path`:"
msgstr ""
